## Approach
Convert to a char array and swap from both ends toward the middle, using two index pointers. No new string or buffer is allocated, so the only extra space is the two int indices.

## Code
```java
public static String reverse(String s) {
    char[] c = s.toCharArray();
    int i = 0, j = c.length - 1;
    while (i < j) {
        char t = c[i];
        c[i] = c[j];
        c[j] = t;
        i++;
        j--;
    }
    return new String(c);
}
```

## Complexity
Time O(n), one pass over half the array. Space O(1) extra beyond the char array, which is the input's own mutable copy.

One caveat: `String` is immutable in Java, so a truly in-place reverse needs a `char[]` or `StringBuilder` you own. If the signature takes `char[]`, drop the `toCharArray` and `new String` and swap directly on the passed array.

```cpp
int binarySearch(const vector<int>& arr, int target) {
    int lo = 0, hi = arr.size() - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (arr[mid] == target) return mid;
        if (arr[mid] < target) lo = mid + 1;
        else hi = mid - 1;
    }
    return -1;
}
```

`lo + (hi - lo) / 2` avoids overflow that `(lo + hi) / 2` can hit on large indices. The loop narrows the range by half each pass and returns the index, or -1 if the target isn't present.

Time O(log n), space O(1).

## Approach
Iterate from 1 to n. For each number, check divisibility by 15 first (both 3 and 5), then by 3, then by 5, otherwise print the number itself. Checking 15 first avoids printing "Fizz" or "Buzz" alone on multiples of both.

## Code
```go
package main

import (
	"fmt"
	"strconv"
	"strings"
)

func fizzBuzz(n int) []string {
	result := make([]string, 0, n)
	for i := 1; i <= n; i++ {
		switch {
		case i%15 == 0:
			result = append(result, "FizzBuzz")
		case i%3 == 0:
			result = append(result, "Fizz")
		case i%5 == 0:
			result = append(result, "Buzz")
		default:
			result = append(result, strconv.Itoa(i))
		}
	}
	return result
}

func main() {
	fmt.Println(strings.Join(fizzBuzz(15), " "))
}
```

## Complexity
Time is O(n), one pass over the range. Space is O(n) for the result slice, or O(1) extra if you print instead of collecting.

